ABSTRACT
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
The Serial Documenting Architecture Research (SDAR) provides a novel,
court-admissible method and system for real-time forensic monitoring and
logging of a guest virtual machine (Kali Purple, kernel 6.16.8, under VMware
Workstation on a Windows 10 host) using a single VMware named-pipe serial
channel mapped to guest device /dev/ttyS0. All bidirectional traffic is
multiplexed over one pipe using RFC 2217 COM-PORT-OPTION semantics emulated
entirely in the host guard application. The Windows 10 host retains sole input
authority (interactive terminal and injection override mode); the Kali VM
operates as a passive watcher with read-only access to the serial channel.

Forensic logs are captured memory-direct from the named pipe into independent
EnCase EWF .01 evidence containers:
  [E01-A] Serial stream evidence item â€” every byte transiting ttyS0
  [E01-B] Host physical memory       â€” WinPmem acquisition wrapped in EWF

Both evidence items carry embedded MD5 and SHA-256 hashes per EWF specification,
case metadata, examiner attribution, and UTC/PST acquisition timestamps. A
SHA-256 hash-chained session log provides per-entry tamper detection. This
architecture satisfies SWGDE, ACPO, ISO 27037, and Daubert/Frye evidentiary
standards for expert witness admissibility.


FIELD OF THE INVENTION
