This invention relates to digital forensics, virtual machine introspection,
serial communications security, evidence acquisition, and chain-of-custody
preservation in hypervisor environments. Specifically, it addresses forensic
monitoring of VMware Workstation guest VMs from Windows host machines using
virtual serial named-pipe channels as evidence collection infrastructure.


BACKGROUND
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
Conventional VM serial monitoring requires either dual serial ports (ttyS0 +
ttyS1) introducing additional attack surface and synchronization complexity, or
non-forensic console scraping methods that are inadmissible as expert witness
evidence. Existing frameworks â€” VMware Tools serial redirect, QEMU serial
consoles, Hyper-V data exchange â€” lack integrated EWF .01 acquisition,
host-only input enforcement, and memory-direct pipe logging.

Individual prior art components (RFC 2217 telnet-serial tunneling, Volatility
live memory analysis, WinPmem acquisition, libewf containers) exist in
isolation. No prior system or method combines them into a tamper-evident,
single-pipe, dual-guard architecture with court-ready EWF output and enforced
unidirectional input authority.

The inventor, Christopher T. Williams, identified this gap during advanced Blue
Team security operations in March 2026 and conceived the complete SDAR solution
as a unified forensic architecture, reduced to practice in the same session.


SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
SDAR comprises the following integrated components:

1. SINGLE NAMED-PIPE SERIAL CHANNEL
   VMware serial port configured as named-pipe server on Windows host
   (\\.\pipe\KaliSerialGuard), mapped to /dev/ttyS0 in the Kali guest.
   Single pipe carries bidirectional traffic without secondary device.

2. RFC 2217 CONTROL EMULATION (HOST SIDE)
   Host guard application negotiates COM-PORT-OPTION (code 0x2C) including
   SET-BAUD-RATE, SET-DATASIZE, SET-PARITY, SET-STOPSIZE, SET-CONTROL.
   Break/interrupt injection implemented via RFC 2217 BREAK-STATE subnegotiation.
   No RFC 2217 daemon required on guest â€” host emulates full control plane.

3. HOST-ONLY INPUT AUTHORITY
   Windows host guard provides:
     a) Interactive terminal mode â€” keystrokes flow in real time to VM
     b) INJECT: override mode â€” host pushes arbitrary command strings
     c) BREAK command â€” RFC 2217 interrupt signal injection
   Guest guard script is architecturally restricted to output-only; it never
   writes to ttyS0 except in response to explicit host-authorized commands.

4. DUAL EWF .01 FORENSIC EVIDENCE ACQUISITION
   [E01-A] Serial stream: named-pipe byte stream captured directly from
           Windows kernel memory-mapped pipe object, written to raw staging
           file, then wrapped by ewfacquire as EnCase EWF .01 evidence item
           with embedded case metadata, examiner name, MD5 + SHA-256 hashes.
   [E01-B] Host memory: WinPmem acquires Windows 10 host physical RAM as
           raw image (Volatility 3 compatible), wrapped independently by
           ewfacquire as separate EWF .01 evidence item.
   Both items are independent forensic objects shareable with EnCase, FTK,
   X-Ways Forensics, and Volatility 3 without format conversion.

5. HASH-CHAINED SESSION LOG
   Each log entry is hashed as: SHA-256(previous_entry_hash || entry_content)
   Producing an append-only tamper-evident chain. Any modification to any
   historical entry invalidates all subsequent hashes, detectable by
   verification against the chain tail hash recorded in EWF metadata.

6. PIPE-FIRST ARCHITECTURE (BOOT TIMING SOLUTION)
   The Windows host guard creates and holds the named pipe SERVER before any
   VM is powered on. VMware connects as client on VM boot. This eliminates
   the pipe instantiation race condition inherent in all prior implementations.


DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF PREFERRED EMBODIMENT
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€

Â§1 â€” Hypervisor Layer (VMware Workstation)
  Serial Port 1: Use named pipe
  Pipe name    : \\.\pipe\KaliSerialGuard
  This end     : The server (client checked in host guard, not VMware)
  Other end    : An application
  Yield CPU    : Enabled

  NOTE: The host guard PowerShell application creates the actual pipe SERVER
  via System.IO.Pipes.NamedPipeServerStream with SYSTEM+Administrators ACL.
  VMware connects to this pre-existing pipe on VM power-on.

Â§2 â€” Windows Host Guard (SerialGuard-Host.ps1)
  - Creates NamedPipeServerStream with PipeSecurity ACL
  - Calls WaitForConnection() â€” blocks until VMware connects on VM boot
  - Sends RFC 2217 COM-PORT-OPTION WILL + subnegotiations (baud/bits/parity)
  - Spawns background input listener thread reading operator console
  - Main loop: reads VM output bytes â†’ writes to raw evidence file +
    hash-chain log; reads operator input â†’ dispatches INJECT/BREAK/MEMACQ/QUIT
  - On QUIT or exception: calls ewfacquire on raw file â†’ E01-A finalized

Â§3 â€” Kali VM Passive Guard (sdar-vm-guard.py)
  - Opens /dev/ttyS0 raw, 115200/8N1, non-blocking via POSIX termios
  - select() loop reads host-injected bytes with 1-second timeout
  - Processes SDAR:STATUS / SDAR:PROCS / SDAR:NETSTAT commands from host
  - Emits configurable heartbeat to ttyS0 (output direction only)
  - Hash-chained log to /var/log/sdar/
  - Deployed as systemd service: sdar-vm-guard.service

Â§4 â€” GRUB Configuration (LUKS-Safe)
  GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT includes: console=tty0 console=ttyS0,115200n8
  tty0 listed FIRST â€” LUKS encrypted volume password prompt remains on
  physical display. ttyS0 receives console output post-LUKS unlock only.
  This is the critical ordering that prevents LUKS boot stall on ttyS0.

Â§5 â€” EWF Acquisition Pipeline
  Serial stream (E01-A):
    ewfacquire -t Serial.E01 -C <case> -e <examiner> -m logical
               -c deflate -f encase6 -u <raw_staging_file>

  Host memory (E01-B):
    winpmem_mini_x64_rc2.exe <raw_output.raw>
    ewfacquire -t HostRAM.E01 -C <case> -e <examiner> -m memory
               -c deflate -f encase6 -u <raw_output.raw>

  Verification:
    ewfverify Serial.E01
    ewfverify HostRAM.E01
    Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 <raw_staging_file>  [cross-check]


CLAIMS
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
Claim 1.
  A computer-implemented method for forensically sound, court-admissible
  monitoring of a guest virtual machine from a host system, comprising:
    (a) creating a named inter-process communication pipe on the host system
        prior to powering on the guest virtual machine;
    (b) mapping said pipe to a virtual serial device in the guest via a
        hypervisor serial port configuration;
    (c) emulating RFC 2217 COM-PORT-OPTION control semantics on the host
        to negotiate baud rate, data bits, parity, stop bits, and flow
        control without a corresponding daemon on the guest;
    (d) enforcing host-only input authority such that the guest system
        cannot write to the serial channel except in authorized output
        directions;
    (e) capturing the entire bidirectional serial byte stream from a
        kernel memory-mapped pipe object directly into an EnCase EWF .01
        format evidence container with embedded cryptographic hashes; and
    (f) producing a separate EWF .01 evidence container containing a
        forensic image of host physical memory acquired contemporaneously.

Claim 2.
  The method of Claim 1, wherein the host guard application provides both
  an interactive terminal mode passing operator keystrokes to the guest in
  real time, and an injection override mode enabling the operator to push
  arbitrary command strings to the guest serial channel independently of
  interactive state.

Claim 3.
  The method of Claim 1, further comprising injecting a break/interrupt
  signal to the guest serial device by transmitting an RFC 2217
  SET-CONTROL BREAK-STATE subnegotiation sequence over the named pipe,
  without requiring a physical serial port, UART, or modem control line.

Claim 4.
  The method of Claim 1, wherein the EWF .01 serial evidence container
  is produced from a raw byte staging file populated by real-time pipe
  reads, and the EWF .01 memory evidence container is produced by piping
  WinPmem physical memory acquisition output through ewfacquire, such
  that both evidence items are independently verifiable via MD5 and
  SHA-256 hash comparison without dependency on each other.

Claim 5.
  The method of Claim 1, further comprising maintaining a SHA-256
  hash-chained session log in which each entry's hash is computed as
  SHA-256(previous_entry_hash concatenated with current_entry_content),
  such that any retroactive modification of any entry invalidates all
  subsequent chain entries, enabling tamper detection by comparison of
  computed chain tail hash with the recorded terminal hash.

Claim 6.
  A system comprising:
    (a) a Windows 10 host guard application implementing Claims 1â€“5 as
        a PowerShell service with SYSTEM-privilege named pipe server,
        RFC 2217 negotiation engine, dual EWF acquisition pipeline,
        hash-chained logger, and interactive/injection input authority;
    (b) a Kali Linux guest passive guard service implementing read-only
        serial monitoring, host command processing, and hash-chained
        local logging on /dev/ttyS0;
    (c) a VMware Workstation hypervisor serial port configured to connect
        said host pipe to said guest device; and
    (d) a GRUB bootloader configuration preserving LUKS encrypted volume
        password prompts on the physical display while routing post-unlock
        console output to the serial channel.

Claim 7.
  The system of Claim 6, wherein the host guard pipe server is created
  and placed in a waiting state before the guest virtual machine is
  powered on, such that the named pipe object exists in the Windows
  kernel object namespace prior to VMware's pipe client connection
  attempt, thereby eliminating boot-sequence timing race conditions
  inherent in alternative implementations.


ADVANTAGES OVER PRIOR ART
â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€
1. Single-pipe eliminates dual-device synchronization complexity and
   attack surface introduced by ttyS0+ttyS1 architectures.
2. EWF .01 native output enables direct import into EnCase, FTK, and
   X-Ways without format conversion, preserving forensic chain of custody.
3. Host-only input authority is architecturally enforced, not policy-
   enforced, preventing guest-to-host command injection vectors.
4. Memory-direct pipe acquisition (not console scraping) satisfies live
   system forensic best practices per NIST SP 800-86.
5. Boot-sequence race condition eliminated by pipe-first architecture.
6. LUKS-safe GRUB configuration preserves encrypted volume integrity
   while enabling full post-unlock serial console forensics.
7. RFC 2217 control emulation on host eliminates guest-side daemon
   requirements, reducing guest attack surface.


